WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.040
Hello again, it’s good to have you back.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
And yes, we really do mean you.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.200
We’re aware that you watch a lot of these&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.240
videos, drinking in all&nbsp;
that classified information

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.400
about some of the SCP Foundation’s most
top secret anomalies.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.120
You probably think you’re pretty slick,
don’t you?

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.640
As if nobody could see you peering in, and
even if they could, they wouldn’t remember

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.000
it soon after?

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.280
Yes, you’re a very clever anomaly, we wouldn’t
even think of arguing against that.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.960
That’s why this video is about you - SCP-3393.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.440
Don’t be alarmed, we’re on the same page
here.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.880
We know that you access these files - particularly
the files regarding yourself, and your own

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:43.840
activities.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.560
We know that you’re on the shy side, and
we definitely appreciate that - unlike our

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.120
other “Shy Guy” - you don’t brutally
murder everyone who witnesses you.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.000
Your greatest crime is accessing and even
tampering with SCP Foundation files without

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
permission, but seeing as the Foundation is
cold, not cruel, we think we can forgive that

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.800
little indiscretion of yours for the greater
good.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.680
All we ask for is your attention, and a few
minutes of your time.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:17.280
You’re a vain creature, who or whatever
you are, so we know you wouldn’t ever resist

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.800
the opportunity to hear what we have to say
about you.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.160
And for the record, you are one of the few
people who are actually witnessing this video

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.640
the way you’re witnessing it right now.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.600
It’s for your eyes only, so to speak, SCP-3393.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.080
This video, its description, and even the
coding of its comment section is filled with

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.840
state-of-the-art memetic agents cooked up
by our best and brightest.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:47.360
To everyone except you, the researcher heading
your case, the head of the Antimemetics Division,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.600
and a single member of the O5 Council, this
is just your average run of the mill video.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.440
This is a private conversation between you
and the Foundation, SCP-3393.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.240
We hope you don’t mind the designation,
it’s not like we know your real name, but

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.520
we can make some rather educated guesses.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.880
We actually know very little about you, on
the whole… a fact we hope to change in future.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.480
Much like several other anomalies we have
files on, such as SCP-055, the Self-Keeping

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:25.680
Secret, and SCP-2256, the Very Tall Things,
information recorded about you isn’t exactly

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.800
sustainable.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.360
It becomes forgotten, or unreadable, shortly
after it’s noted down.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.200
It’s a clever anomalous ability - and one
we’ve only been able to counteract with

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.160
the application of powerful Mnestic Drugs.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.840
But clearly, that isn’t the only thing you
have going for you.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.960
How do you think you’ve been able to access
all of these videos, filled with classified

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.160
information about anomalies, groups of interest,
and even the inner workings of the Foundation

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.120
itself?

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.240
Through methods currently unknown to us, you’ve
been able to somehow give your account Level

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.520
4 Clearance, giving you free reign over almost
every file available.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.640
As much as it shames us to admit it, we haven’t
been able to remove this unpermitted clearance

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.320
from your account, or even locate it, for
that matter.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.480
Like we said before, you’re a very clever
anomaly.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.280
We respect that about you, even though you’ve
been using this apparently crafty sapience

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.960
to inconvenience us.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.920
Our best guess is that you owe much of your
success to powerful antimemetic properties

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.840
inherent to your anomalous being, but if we’re
off the mark on that, do feel free to enlighten

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:34.480
us.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.560
Speaking of guesses, let’s get back to what
we think we know about you.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.520
After all, that’s why you’re really here,
isn’t it?

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.720
No point being bashful, SCP-3393.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.200
You may be good at hiding yourself, but your
intentions are as plain as day.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.040
You don’t need to be a senior researcher
here to intuit that much about you.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.240
The following are all facts we believe we
know about you with 90% confidence, you’ll

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.040
have to let us know how close we were in the
end.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.480
Assumption number one: You are a corporeal
entity.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.240
That’s right - you probably wanted us to
assume you were just some kind of digital

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.600
ghost, didn’t you?

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.720
Some mysterious, ethereal thought-form existing
only in the realm of cyberspace, while you

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.360
sneak around Site-31 making mischief.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.040
We’re afraid that that ghost has been given
up, so to speak.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.600
As clever as you are, it isn’t quite as
clever as you believe yourself to be.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.640
You’ve gotten sloppy at times, and in these
moments, you’ve given us a wealth of evidence.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.360
Don’t you think the Foundation researchers
and guards - people trained to notice even

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.240
the most minute anomalous interference - wouldn’t
notice doors opening and closing on their

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:40.720
own?

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:41.760
Equipment moving?

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.360
Computer terminals activated, despite no digital
trace of approved Foundation login credentials?

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.000
We must say, we’re a little insulted by
how much you’ve underestimated us here.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.080
We know the files you’ve accessed; we know
that you’re aware of some of the monstrosities

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.880
the Foundation deals with: Ghosts, deadly
viruses, reality warpers, creatures that can

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.720
shapeshift or become invisible.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.200
Deadly memes, for goodness sake!

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.000
Did you really think our personnel would see
the signs of your meddling and think, “Eh,

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.280
it was probably nothing?”

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.480
That’s a rather dangerous oversight on your
part.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.480
Assumption number two: You’re attracted
to information about yourself.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.920
Don’t try to deny this one, SCP-3393, the
fact you’re still watching this video is

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.160
proof of your self-obsession.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.800
And you’ve left your digital fingerprints
all over any other file that made the mistake

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.080
of mentioning you.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:38.080
We have pages upon pages of logs, showing
your long record of unauthorized access, and

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.240
the grand majority of the files you accessed
were about… you.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.040
Like we said, you’re a vain little creature,
that much is plain to see.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.280
This leads us nicely onto…

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.320
Assumption number three: You seek out this
information about yourself in order to make

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.880
changes to it, or even delete it entirely
if it displeases you.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.160
We’ll admit, this is the one we find the
most frustrating.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.320
It’s one thing to access the files without
our permission, quite another to mess with

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:07.440
our hard work.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.000
We get enough problems from Technical Director
Rosen already, without needing to constantly

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.640
pester him about improving file security.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.520
The file about you has undergone a number
of needless modifications and deletions.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:21.520
The following was a personal favorite.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:22.160
Quote:

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:23.760
“Object Class: Euclid

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.600
Special Containment Procedures: Don't worry,
I can handle this.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.080
Description: I wasn't supposed to happen.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.920
I wasn't supposed to exist.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.680
At least not like this.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.120
But, let me deal with it.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.160
This thing that I am is best kept away from
all of you.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.120
Don't try to analyze me, or test me, or whatever.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.160
I don't want any of you to catch what I have.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.960
Just call me self-containing and we'll be
good.”

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:47.840
End quote.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.800
We suppose you thought that one was pretty
funny, right?

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.840
You’re clearly an intelligent creature,
who or whatever you are, but the clear arrogance

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.840
on display here doesn’t suit you.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.400
While you may coast off of your antimemetic
properties, it’s important to remember that

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.400
you aren’t untouchable.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:05.680
Speaking of which…

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.520
Assumption number four:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.560
Your antimemetic properties
can be transferred.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.400
We found this to be a particularly interesting
wrinkle in your story.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.480
We know you’ve got a sense of humor, so
you have to appreciate the irony in your attempts

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.600
to alter or destroy information about yourself
telling us more about you than we ever knew

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:24.640
before.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.880
Initially, when other files were reported
missing, we thought you must have been deleting

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.160
them out of spite.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:35.040
However, upon further examination, we found
that the files were still very much there,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.000
and you were the last user to access them.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.360
This is perhaps the most dangerous thing about
you, and we don’t even know if you knew

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:44.720
it until now.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.760
Your antimemetic properties have a tendency
to rub off and affect the things you interact

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:50.240
with.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:55.200
With a power like that, you could compromise
the whole database, and obviously, we can’t

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.960
allow that to happen.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:02.560
Assumption number five: You have a strong
resistance to memetic agents, much to our

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:03.600
frustration.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.360
We know that they can affect you, of course,
but your tolerance is far higher than that

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.120
of the average human being.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.880
We needed to load this video with some of
our very best, just to make sure it would

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:14.000
actually work.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.280
Your memetic resistance has even foiled certain
prior containment attempts, so you’ve really

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.960
been making us earn our keep, SCP-3393.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.400
Assumption number six: You are confined to
Site-31.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.840
This is another inconvenient truth for you,
isn’t it?

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.080
First, we figure out you’re a corporeal
being, existing out here in space-time with

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.800
the rest of us, and then, we figure out exactly
which space and time you’re occupying.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.080
Can you feel the net tightening around you,
3393?

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.720
Of course, we don’t want you to be nervous,
just sit tight and enjoy the video.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.120
You’re a very clever anomaly, after all.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.600
Far too clever to be contained by the likes
of us.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.040
You’ve been performing your operations right
under the noses of Site-31’s most talented

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.000
personnel.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.480
All the phenomena we’ve been attributing
to you - from the opened doors to the tampered

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.640
computer terminals - has occurred exclusively
in Site-31.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:11.040
And while we may not be able to track down
your account and strip it of its phony clearances,

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:17.680
did you know we can log which computer terminals
unauthorized access to our files comes from?

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.920
That’s just basic cyber-security.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.760
And we don’t need to tell you that all of
your unauthorized accesses have been logged

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.760
to Site-31 terminals.

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:28.160
Very sloppy, 3393.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.040
Or should we call you Dr. Mackey?

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.440
Forgive us if that isn’t your name, we hate
to jump to conclusions here at the Foundation,

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.600
but we couldn’t help but notice that your&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.400
anomalous activity began&nbsp;
shortly after a researcher

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.680
named Dr. Mackey disappeared from Site-31.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.960
Interestingly, much like you, Dr. Mackey isn’t
remembered by anyone else on the site, we

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.280
only know Dr. Mackey worked here from records
we discovered not too long ago.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.480
We know that Dr. Mackey was conducting experiments
here - though data on the particulars of those

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.920
experiments have been destroyed, by you, we
presume.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:07.120
We also presume that you are most likely either
Dr. Mackey, or the product of one of Dr. Mackey’s

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:08.240
experiments.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.880
We’ve made a few attempts to nab you already,
but you’ve always slipped through the cracks.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:18.240
Like the sweep of Site-31 where we sent in
MTF Eta-10, “See No Evil”, our team that

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.360
specializes in combating visual cognitohazards.

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.480
Those guys are tough cookies, and they’re
damn good at their job, so it’s a mystery

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:27.840
how you evaded them.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:33.440
Our current best guess is that you were hiding
in some undocumented location within the Site.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.600
Our attempts at memetic incapacitation were
equally unsuccessful.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:41.840
As we mentioned before, it’s clear you have
a greater resilience to memetic agents than

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.240
any of your non-anomalous counterparts.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:48.480
We estimate that even a kill agent would probably
do little more than weaken you.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:51.200
That’s impressive, we’ll give you that.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:56.800
Our estimation for why you’ve been able
to so handily thwart our attempts at containing

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:02.640
you is that your access to the system has
also given you access to all of our plans.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.600
You know exactly what’s going on before
we even execute the attempt, giving you time

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.560
to think up and execute an&nbsp;
effective countermeasure.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:16.960
But we here at the Foundation believe that
knowledge is power, SCP-3393, and we have

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.440
more than enough to finally contain you.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:24.240
Don’t panic, it’s already essentially
over, but we’ll do you the courtesy of explaining

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.080
how we got you in the end.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:28.400
Welcome to Operation Bait the Hook.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:33.600
We know you’re within Site-31, and we know
you’re attracted to information about yourself,

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:38.240
so we limited the scope of where this handy
little video could be viewed to five terminals

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:39.120
in the site.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:44.320
We put measures in place to notify us whenever
the file was accessed, and from which terminal,

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:49.360
at which point MTF Eta-10 would be dispatched
to seal off all available exits and quarantine

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.040
the room around the terminal.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.760
A process that would take only several minutes.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:59.280
Just enough time for a vain little creature
to watch a video about itself, as it’s forever

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:03.040
entombed with the telltale terminal that finally
gave it away.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:07.520
You wouldn’t be brought into a containment
chamber, so we simply brought the containment

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:08.880
chamber to you.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:13.600
As the network connections for this particular
terminal are shut off, we can safely say you

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.480
won’t be causing us problems anymore.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:20.800
You’re a very clever anomaly, SCP-3393.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:23.520
Just not quite as clever as us.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:30.480
Now go check out “SCP-055 - Anti Meme / Unknown”
and “SCP-3999 - I Am At The Center of Everything

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.520
That Happens To Me” for more mind-bending
anomalies from SCP Explained!

